        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The reference class is the 14 official annual prints from April 2025 through May 2026. Its simple persistence base rate is the latest 4.0% print, but July 2025's unusually large 1.3% monthly increase will leave the twelve-month comparison. Assuming roughly 0.3% monthly inflation in both June and July 2026, June 2025's 0.1% base adds about 0.2 point before July 2025's 1.3% base subtracts about 1.0 point, implying approximately 3.2%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level and momentum remain firm because May annual CPI was 4.0% and Housing was 6.5%. The principal one-off is the July 2025 base effect; easing Transport inflation reinforces the decline. Policy works only with a lag over this horizon, so no separate large policy adjustment is imposed."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence model prior = 4.0%, using the 14-print April 2025\u2013May 2026 official history. Adjustment components are +0.2 percentage point for replacing June 2025's 0.1% monthly rise with an assumed 0.3%, then -1.0 point for replacing July 2025's 1.3% rise with an assumed 0.3%: 4.0 + 0.2 - 1.0 = 3.2%. Successive annual-rate changes are -0.3, -0.2, +1.1, +0.2, +0.4, +0.2, -0.4, +0.4, 0.0, -0.1, +0.9, -0.4, and -0.2 percentage point; their sample standard deviation is sigma = 0.476. The empirical 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 0.610, giving 3.2 \u00b1 0.61 = [2.59, 3.81], rounded to [2.6%, 3.8%]."
      },
